{:edb/db-before-t 2229 :edb/db-after-t 2230 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535542 50 #inst "2026-10-09T02:09:08.958Z" 13194139535542 true] [17592186124269 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067114/assets/7d82125089d15dada1be4f8d6669b5e125da921019b3a9d27376353dcf59a090.png)\n\nSuppose $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ are bases of the vector spaces $V$ and $W,$ respectively. The matrix $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}|\\mathcal{C}}$ of the linear map $\\mathbf{T}:V \\rightarrow W$ relative to these bases, and the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in V$ relative to $\\mathcal{B}$ are shown above. Find $[\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{x})]_{\\mathcal{C}}.$" 13194139535542 true] [17592186124269 1100 "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSuppose $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ are bases of the vector spaces $V$ and $W$, respectively. The matrix $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B} \\mid \\mathcal{C}}$ of the linear map $\\mathbf{T}:V \\rightarrow W$ relative to these bases, and the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in V$ relative to $\\mathcal{B}$ are shown above. Find $[\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{x})]_{\\mathcal{C}}$." 13194139535542 false] [17592186124269 1145 "The coordinates of the image of $\\mathbf{x}$ under $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$ can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[T(x)]_{C} &= [T]_{B \\mid C} \\cdot [x]_{B} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535542 false] [17592186124269 1145 "The coordinates of the image of $\\mathbf{x}$ under $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$ can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{C} & =[T]_{B|C}\\cdot [x]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535542 true] [17592186124270 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067114/assets/8caf13cdbf21ddfb1a121ede6ec0c21da155065905547bbd86b0e52cd57141da.png)\n\nConsider the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ (shown above) and a basis $\\mathcal{C}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\nThe matrix $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}|\\mathcal{C}}$ of the linear map $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^2 \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^3$ relative to these bases, and the vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ are also given. Find $[\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{x})]_{\\mathcal{C}}.$" 13194139535542 true] [17592186124270 1100 "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ (shown above) and a basis $\\mathcal{C}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^3$.\nThe matrix $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B} \\mid \\mathcal{C}}$ of the linear map $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^2 \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^3$ relative to these bases, and the vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, are also given. Find $[\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{x})]_{\\mathcal{C}}$." 13194139535542 false] [17592186124270 1145 "We can find the coordinates of the image of $\\mathbf{x}$ under $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[T(x)]_{C} &= [T]_{B \\mid C} \\cdot [x]_{B}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}$, the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to $\\mathcal{B}$. So, let\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the system\n\n$$\n{x_{1} - 2x_{2} = 5; 4x_{1} - 5x_{2} = 2\n$$\n\nWe now row-reduce the corresponding augmented matrix using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 4 & -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2}:=R_{2} + (-4)R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -18\\end{bmatrix}\\end{vmatrix}R_{2}:=\\frac{1}{3}R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{1}:=R_{1} + 2R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[T(x)]_{C} &= [T]_{B \\mid C} \\cdot [x]_{B} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}17 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535542 false] [17592186124270 1145 "We can find the coordinates of the image of $\\mathbf{x}$ under $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{C} & =[T]_{B|C}\\cdot [x]_{B}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}},$ the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to $\\mathcal{B}.$ So, let\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067114/assets/a5b6f5f41dffd99a885bb872eac6f1e6a24bd604b360256194a0bf16b32ef381.png)\n\nThis gives us the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=5 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now row-reduce the corresponding augmented matrix using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 4 & -5 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -18\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067114/assets/862742d57ad0ae4b56d6f345d825313bebf0d712f9c59ea1ed8b55d0a2eea22e.png)\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{C} & =[T]_{B|C}\\cdot [x]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}17 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535542 true] [17592186124333 1100 "Suppose $\\mathbf{T} : V \\rightarrow W$ is a linear map and $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2, \\mathbf{b}_3 \\}$ and $\\mathcal{C} = \\{ \\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2 \\}$ are the bases of the vector spaces $V$ and $W,$ respectively. Find the matrix $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}|\\mathcal{C}}$ of the map $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to these bases, if \n\n$\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_1)=-3\\mathbf{c}_1+5\\mathbf{c}_2,\\quad$ $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_2)=\\mathbf{c}_2-2\\mathbf{c}_1\\quad$ and $\\quad\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_3)=\\mathbf{c}_1+5\\mathbf{c}_2.$" 13194139535542 true] [17592186124333 1100 "Suppose $\\mathbf{T}: V \\rightarrow W$ is a linear map and $\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2, \\mathbf{b}_3 \\}$ and $\\mathcal{C} = \\{\\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2 \\}$ are the bases of the vector spaces $V$ and $W$, respectively. Find the matrix $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B} \\mid \\mathcal{C}}$ of the map $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to these bases, if\n\n> $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_1)=-3\\mathbf{c}_1+5\\mathbf{c}_2,\\quad$ $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_2)=\\mathbf{c}_2-2\\mathbf{c}_1\\quad$ and $\\quad\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_3)=\\mathbf{c}_1+5\\mathbf{c}_2$." 13194139535542 false] [17592186124333 1145 "First, we write the images of vectors in $\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C} \\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({b}_{1})=-3{c}_{1}+5{c}_{2} & \\,⟹\\,[T({b}_{1})]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({b}_{2})={c}_{2}-2{c}_{1} & \\,⟹\\,[T({b}_{2})]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({b}_{3})={c}_{1}+5{c}_{2} & \\,⟹\\,[T({b}_{3})]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}|\\mathcal{C}}$ is the matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_1),$ $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_2)$ and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_3)$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067114/assets/215cd69925ccfeab3ccb20874b7cdc2d837b99f33d8112f95fa5d2b646a27d40.png)" 13194139535542 true] [17592186124333 1145 "First, we write the images of vectors in $\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}T(b_{1}) =-3c_{1} + 5c_{2} & ⟹[T(b_{1})]_{C} = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T(b_{2}) = c_{2} - 2c_{1} & ⟹[T(b_{2})]_{C} = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T(b_{3}) = c_{1} + 5c_{2} & ⟹[T(b_{3})]_{C} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B} \\mid \\mathcal{C}}$ is the matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_1)$, $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_2)$ and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_3)$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139535542 false] [17592186124334 1100 "Consider the basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2, \\mathbf{b}_3\\}$ of the vector space $V$ and the basis $\\mathcal{C}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067114/assets/7c123e6f7d842a8e86e4c99826d25df070982514960752a25481a9f3f046cc1b.png)\n\nFind the matrix $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}|\\mathcal{C}}$ of the map $\\mathbf{T}:V \\to \\mathbb{R}^2$ relative to these bases if \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067114/assets/9a469219c661453d7891935b2ef0a32bff972bcf903d641ca7e31b7bec26369a.png)" 13194139535542 true] [17592186124334 1100 "Consider the basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2, \\mathbf{b}_3\\}$ of the vector space $V$ and the basis $\\mathcal{C}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFind the matrix $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B} \\mid \\mathcal{C}}$ of the map $\\mathbf{T}:V \\to \\mathbb{R}^2$ relative to these bases if\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535542 false] [17592186124334 1145 "Recall that $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B} \\mid \\mathcal{C}}$ is the matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_1)$, $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_2)$, and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_3)$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$.\n\nFirst, we need to find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_i)$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$ for each $i=1,2,3$. We can do this simultaneously by considering the following matrix:\n\n$$\n\\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid; c_{1}\\begin{vmatrix}c_{2} & T(b_{1}) & T(b_{2})\\end{vmatrix}T(b_{3}); \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid\n$$\n\nWe reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 & 1 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 2 & -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{1}:=(-1)R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & -1 & 3 & -7 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 2 & -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{vmatrix}R_{2}:=R_{2} + (-1)R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & -1 & 3 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -8 & 8\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{1}:=R_{1} + 4R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 11 & -29 & 25 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -8 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the right-hand side gives the matrix of the linear transformation relative to the bases $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -29 & 25 \\\\ 3 & -8 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139535542 false] [17592186124334 1145 "Recall that $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}|\\mathcal{C}}$ is the matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_1),$ $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_2),$ and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_3)$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_i)$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$ for each $i=1,2,3.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the following matrix:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067114/assets/9d774b5bd79f14bc31e88a649fcb2a682d94ae3dd8acde5e25f52369c136e11e.png)\n\nWe reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & 1 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 2 & -5 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & -1 & 3 & -7 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 2 & -5 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & -1 & 3 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -8 & 8\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+4{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 11 & -29 & 25 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -8 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the right-hand side gives the matrix of the linear transformation relative to the bases $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067114/assets/778f179c4681bcdcc5a64bcd128d946a272fa869289d2c2e542df75ad05850b6.png)" 13194139535542 true] [17592186124463 1020 #edb/ref 17592186354380 13194139535542 true] [17592186124463 1020 #edb/ref 17592186354413 13194139535542 true] [17592186124463 1020 #edb/ref 17592186354427 13194139535542 true] [17592186124463 1020 #edb/ref 17592186354462 13194139535542 true] [17592186124463 1020 #edb/ref 17592186354469 13194139535542 true] [17592186124464 1020 #edb/ref 17592186354387 13194139535542 true] [17592186124464 1020 #edb/ref 17592186354476 13194139535542 true] [17592186124464 1020 #edb/ref 17592186354483 13194139535542 true] [17592186124464 1020 #edb/ref 1759